1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,687
In the cool morning, Aarav's little
speaker plays a song clearly.

2
00:00:03,987 --> 00:00:07,789
By the hot afternoon, the same
song sounds harsh and broken.

3
00:00:08,089 --> 00:00:09,162
Nobody touched it.

4
00:00:09,162 --> 00:00:10,614
Only the room got hotter.

5
00:00:10,914 --> 00:00:12,542
Why does heat spoil the sound?

6
00:00:12,542 --> 00:00:14,131
And how do engineers stop it?

7
00:00:14,631 --> 00:00:17,333
Think of a water tap
that must stay half open.

8
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:21,090
Half open leaves room for the
flow to wiggle up and down.

9
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:25,516
This resting point is called the
bias, or the operating point.

10
00:00:25,866 --> 00:00:28,977
Now imagine heat makes the
tap creep open by itself.

11
00:00:29,327 --> 00:00:30,812
The flow keeps growing.

12
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:33,148
Soon there is no room left to wiggle.

13
00:00:33,498 --> 00:00:35,694
The tops of the waves get cut off.

14
00:00:35,694 --> 00:00:37,275
That is the harsh sound.

15
00:00:37,925 --> 00:00:40,774
The fix is like the float in a water tank.

16
00:00:41,124 --> 00:00:44,830
When the water rises, the float
lifts and closes the inlet.

17
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:46,113
It corrects itself.

18
00:00:46,113 --> 00:00:47,303
No one has to watch it.

19
00:00:47,953 --> 00:00:49,900
Let's test this on the breadboard.

20
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:52,666
First, the simplest way to bias.

21
00:00:52,666 --> 00:00:54,978
One big resistor feeds the base.

22
00:00:55,328 --> 00:00:57,162
This is called fixed bias.

23
00:00:57,162 --> 00:00:58,749
A small wave goes in.

24
00:00:59,099 --> 00:01:01,770
The collector current is about
one point one milliamperes.

25
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:05,798
The scope shows a clean,
upside down copy of the input.

26
00:01:06,148 --> 00:01:10,136
Now we heat the transistor, from
twenty five to seventy five degrees.

27
00:01:10,486 --> 00:01:12,860
Heat raises beta, the current gain.

28
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:14,047
Here it doubles.

29
00:01:14,397 --> 00:01:16,452
The collector current doubles too.

30
00:01:16,452 --> 00:01:18,470
The output sits near the bottom.

31
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:20,000
Look at the scope.

32
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,274
The bottom of the wave is chopped flat.

33
00:01:22,924 --> 00:01:26,467
Now the same transistor
in voltage divider bias.

34
00:01:26,817 --> 00:01:30,340
Two resistors, R one and R
two, set the base voltage.

35
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:34,194
And a new resistor, RE,
sits below the emitter.

36
00:01:34,544 --> 00:01:37,270
Heat it again, from twenty
five to seventy five degrees.

37
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:38,922
Beta doubles again.

38
00:01:38,922 --> 00:01:40,810
But the current hardly moves.

39
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:43,095
The output wave stays clean.

40
00:01:43,095 --> 00:01:45,128
The operating point stays put.

41
00:01:45,778 --> 00:01:47,312
Why does it stay put?

42
00:01:47,312 --> 00:01:48,491
Follow the loop.

43
00:01:48,841 --> 00:01:51,045
Heat pushes the collector current up.

44
00:01:51,395 --> 00:01:54,818
More current through RE
raises the emitter voltage.

45
00:01:55,168 --> 00:01:58,292
But the base voltage is
held fixed by the divider.

46
00:01:58,642 --> 00:02:01,588
So the voltage between
base and emitter shrinks.

47
00:02:01,938 --> 00:02:05,670
Less base current flows, and the
collector current falls back.

48
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:07,381
RE is the float.

49
00:02:07,381 --> 00:02:09,781
It pulls the current back by itself.

50
00:02:10,431 --> 00:02:12,296
Engineers call this negative feedback.

51
00:02:12,646 --> 00:02:14,819
Without it, heat can feed on itself.

52
00:02:15,169 --> 00:02:18,507
More current makes more heat,
and the leakage current grows.

53
00:02:18,857 --> 00:02:22,342
Leakage roughly doubles for
every ten degrees of extra heat.

54
00:02:22,692 --> 00:02:26,046
The current runs away
until the transistor burns.

55
00:02:26,046 --> 00:02:27,829
This is thermal runaway.

56
00:02:28,479 --> 00:02:30,227
The base voltage is held.

57
00:02:30,227 --> 00:02:32,519
The emitter resistor does the rest.

58
00:02:33,319 --> 00:02:35,570
Now the maths, one step at a time.

59
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:38,137
The divider sets the base voltage.

60
00:02:38,487 --> 00:02:43,247
VB equals VCC, times R
two, over R one plus R two.

61
00:02:43,947 --> 00:02:47,294
The emitter sits zero
point seven volts lower.

62
00:02:47,294 --> 00:02:49,659
VE equals VB minus VBE.

63
00:02:50,359 --> 00:02:52,882
Ohm's law on RE gives the current.

64
00:02:52,882 --> 00:02:54,886
IE equals VE over RE.

65
00:02:55,586 --> 00:02:57,763
Beta is nowhere in this equation.

66
00:02:57,763 --> 00:02:59,950
That is why heat cannot move it.

67
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:03,604
The collector current
is almost equal to IE.

68
00:03:04,304 --> 00:03:09,283
So VCE equals VCC, minus
IC, times RC plus RE.

69
00:03:09,983 --> 00:03:13,540
Engineers measure stability
with the stability factor, S.

70
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:18,004
S tells how much the collector
current changes when leakage changes.

71
00:03:18,704 --> 00:03:21,728
Fixed bias has S equal to beta plus one.

72
00:03:22,078 --> 00:03:25,110
Voltage divider bias keeps S close to one.

73
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,763
Smaller S means more stable.

74
00:03:27,763 --> 00:03:29,981
One is the best possible value.

75
00:03:30,631 --> 00:03:31,681
Worked example.

76
00:03:31,681 --> 00:03:33,313
Find the operating point.

77
00:03:33,663 --> 00:03:35,499
VCC is ten volts.

78
00:03:35,499 --> 00:03:37,571
R one is ten kilo ohms.

79
00:03:37,921 --> 00:03:40,387
R two is two point two kilo ohms.

80
00:03:40,737 --> 00:03:43,414
RC is three point six kilo ohms.

81
00:03:43,414 --> 00:03:45,225
RE is one kilo ohm.

82
00:03:45,575 --> 00:03:49,896
VB equals ten, times two point
two, over twelve point two.

83
00:03:50,246 --> 00:03:52,043
That is one point eight volts.

84
00:03:52,743 --> 00:03:54,818
Take away zero point seven.

85
00:03:54,818 --> 00:03:57,025
VE is one point one volts.

86
00:03:57,725 --> 00:04:00,291
One point one volts across one kilo ohm.

87
00:04:00,291 --> 00:04:02,519
IE is one point one milliamperes.

88
00:04:03,219 --> 00:04:08,128
VCE is ten, minus one point one
milliamperes, times four point six kilo

89
00:04:08,128 --> 00:04:09,028
ohms.

90
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:11,571
That is about four point nine volts.

91
00:04:11,571 --> 00:04:13,961
The operating point sits near the middle.

92
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:16,075
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:16,425 --> 00:04:19,527
Many students put the whole
base voltage across RE.

94
00:04:19,877 --> 00:04:21,946
That gives one point eight milliamperes.

95
00:04:21,946 --> 00:04:22,846
Wrong.

96
00:04:22,430 --> 00:04:24,714
First take away the
zero point seven volts.

97
00:04:25,364 --> 00:04:27,254
Only VE sits across RE.

98
00:04:27,254 --> 00:04:29,794
So IE is one point one milliamperes.

99
00:04:30,144 --> 00:04:31,765
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:31,765 --> 00:04:33,625
In PUC board papers.

101
00:04:34,275 --> 00:04:39,309
List the advantages of voltage divider
bias, find the operating point,

102
00:04:39,309 --> 00:04:40,542
and derive S.

103
00:04:41,192 --> 00:04:42,207
Try this.

104
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:48,311
VCC is twelve volts, R one forty
kilo ohms, R two ten kilo ohms, RC

105
00:04:48,311 --> 00:04:51,381
four kilo ohms, RE one kilo ohm.

106
00:04:51,381 --> 00:04:52,668
Find VCE.

107
00:04:58,068 --> 00:04:58,968
B.

108
00:04:58,433 --> 00:04:59,892
Three point five volts.

109
00:05:00,242 --> 00:05:02,883
VB is twelve times ten over fifty.

110
00:05:02,883 --> 00:05:05,076
That is two point four volts.

111
00:05:05,426 --> 00:05:07,531
Take away zero point seven.

112
00:05:07,531 --> 00:05:09,879
VE is one point seven volts.

113
00:05:10,229 --> 00:05:12,439
So IC is one point seven milliamperes.

114
00:05:12,789 --> 00:05:16,710
VCE is twelve, minus one
point seven times five.

115
00:05:16,710 --> 00:05:18,542
Three point five volts.

116
00:05:19,192 --> 00:05:20,738
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:23,938 --> 00:05:25,742
Next, the CE amplifier.

118
00:05:25,742 --> 00:05:29,447
Why does an amplifier boost
some sounds better than others?
